;; The module faulthandler (queue 220; Modules/faulthandler.c and Python/traceback.c's dump functions of CPython 3.14.10,
;; PSF License Agreement): the Python traceback of every thread written to a descriptor, on a fatal signal (enable), after
;; a timeout (dump_traceback_later), on a user signal (register) or when asked (dump_traceback).
;;
;; The threads are pyi's (allThreads, builtins_thread.go): the main thread and each started one, newest first as
;; CPython's interpreter lists its thread states; a thread's frames its Frame chain, read without its lock (a pointer and
;; an instruction index read whole) as CPython reads another thread's frames from a signal handler. A fatal signal another
;; process sends arrives through os/signal on fatalCh; one pyi raises itself (_sigsegv, _sigabrt, os.kill of its own pid,
;; signal.raise_signal: ownSignalHook) runs the handler in the raising thread first, as the kernel runs a synchronous
;; signal's handler. Then the process dies by the signal, its action set to SIG_DFL by rt_sigaction (fhDie: Go's own
;; handler would print a Go traceback). A thread's ident stands for its pthread id; musl has no backtrace(), so the C
;; stack is "<cannot get C stack on this system>" as CPython's on Alpine; no "Extension modules:" line (pyi has none).

